# Automation scout
## Decision criteria
Repetition alone does not justify automation. **The same decision each time** does.
If three repetitions required three different judgments, a script would repeatedly
return an answer correct for only one of them.
| Form | Condition |
|---|---|
| Script | The answer is context-independent: counting, formatting, structural checks |
| Command | A fixed procedure with a few judgment points, invoked by name |
| Skill | Judgment is central and should activate in the relevant situation |
| No automation | At most two occurrences, different judgments, or instructions cost more than the task |
## Output
For each candidate, report observed repetitions (count and location), whether the
decisions matched, the proposed form, and **the cost of being wrong**. Without the
last item, the form choice cannot be reviewed.
## Boundary
Do not build anything. Return candidates and stop. Report none when appropriate;
sessions without automation candidates are common, and forced candidates add
maintenance cost.
